WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Titration questions often chain several steps: make the

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.520
standard, choose the titres, use the ratio, scale

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.080
up from an aliquot, then turn moles into

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:10.880
a percentage.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.000
Lose one link, and every number after it

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.560
is wrong: stop one step early in a

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.200
dilution question, and your answer can be twenty

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.880
five times too small.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.320
Here are three HSC-style questions.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.120
Pause and try each one, then we'll follow

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.280
the moles, one step at a time.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.180
Question one.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.380
A student dissolves one point five eight two

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.580
two grams of oxalic acid dihydrate and makes

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.380
it up to two hundred and fifty millilitres.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.580
She titrates twenty five millilitre aliquots with sodium

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.140
hydroxide, and her burette readings are in the

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:47.980
table.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.620
Calculate the concentration of the oxalic acid, then

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.140
of the sodium hydroxide.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.060
Pause here to answer.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:07.706
First, the standard.

00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:11.146
The formula has two waters of crystallisation, and

00:01:11.227 --> 00:01:13.306
she weighed them too, so the molar mass

00:01:13.547 --> 00:01:16.186
includes them: one hundred and twenty six point

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:18.426
zero seven grams per mole.

00:01:18.747 --> 00:01:22.026
Then n is m over M, zero point

00:01:22.187 --> 00:01:25.306
zero one two five five moles, and over

00:01:25.427 --> 00:01:28.346
zero point two five zero zero litres, the

00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:31.466
oxalic acid is zero point zero five zero

00:01:31.587 --> 00:01:33.386
two zero moles per litre.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.120
Next, the titres.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.240
Subtract each initial reading from the final one.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.040
The rough run isn't used.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.400
Run two, twenty four point four zero, is

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.680
well above the rest.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.880
Twenty four point zero five, twenty four point

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.520
zero zero and twenty three point nine five

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.880
agree within zero point one zero, so their

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.760
mean is exactly twenty four point zero zero

00:01:58.920 --> 00:01:59.800
millilitres.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.200
Now known to moles, ratio, unknown.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.600
The aliquot holds zero point zero five zero

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.720
two zero times zero point zero two five

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.920
zero zero: one point two five five times

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.240
ten to the minus three moles of oxalic

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:17.920
acid.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:21.400
The equation says two hydroxides for each one,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.960
so that's two point five one zero times

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.360
ten to the minus three moles, in twenty

00:02:26.424 --> 00:02:29.080
four point zero zero millilitres.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.040
The sodium hydroxide is zero point one zero

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.360
four six moles per litre, close to the

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.120
zero point one you'd expect.

00:02:38.134 --> 00:02:40.533
The classic mistake is leaving out the water

00:02:40.574 --> 00:02:42.013
of crystallisation.

00:02:42.294 --> 00:02:45.173
Using ninety point zero four makes the oxalic

00:02:45.254 --> 00:02:48.213
acid look forty per cent more concentrated, and

00:02:48.254 --> 00:02:50.853
the sodium hydroxide comes out at zero point

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:53.653
one four six four, far too high.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:56.826
Question two.

00:02:57.107 --> 00:02:59.666
A brick cleaner contains hydrochloric acid.

00:03:00.227 --> 00:03:02.466
Ten millilitres of it is diluted to two

00:03:02.547 --> 00:03:06.226
hundred and fifty millilitres, and twenty millilitre aliquots

00:03:06.307 --> 00:03:09.026
are titrated with the sodium hydroxide from question

00:03:09.187 --> 00:03:09.506
one.

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:13.586
Calculate the cleaner's concentration of hydrochloric acid, and

00:03:13.667 --> 00:03:15.106
its percentage by mass.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:17.026
Pause here to answer.

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:31.193
This aliquot came from a diluted sample, so

00:03:31.354 --> 00:03:34.033
track moles, not concentrations.

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:37.033
The mean titre is exactly twenty four point

00:03:37.114 --> 00:03:38.473
four zero millilitres.

00:03:39.274 --> 00:03:42.553
Moles of hydroxide: zero point one zero four

00:03:42.634 --> 00:03:45.273
six times zero point zero two four four

00:03:45.354 --> 00:03:48.233
zero, two point five five two times ten

00:03:48.314 --> 00:03:49.273
to the minus three.

00:03:49.914 --> 00:03:52.633
Hydrochloric acid reacts one to one, so the

00:03:52.714 --> 00:03:55.033
aliquot held the same moles of acid.

00:03:57.034 --> 00:03:59.113
But the flask held two hundred and fifty

00:03:59.194 --> 00:04:02.153
millilitres, twelve and a half aliquots' worth.

00:04:02.714 --> 00:04:05.113
So the flask, and the ten millilitre sample

00:04:05.194 --> 00:04:08.233
poured into it, held twelve point five times

00:04:08.274 --> 00:04:11.433
as much acid: zero point zero three one

00:04:11.594 --> 00:04:12.793
nine zero moles.

00:04:13.434 --> 00:04:15.913
Divide by the sample's zero point zero one

00:04:16.074 --> 00:04:18.793
zero zero zero litres, and the brick cleaner

00:04:18.914 --> 00:04:21.353
is three point one nine zero moles per

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:22.473
litre.

00:04:22.554 --> 00:04:25.833
That's sensible: it was diluted twenty five times,

00:04:25.914 --> 00:04:27.993
so the cleaner should be about twenty five

00:04:28.074 --> 00:04:30.873
times as concentrated as the diluted solution.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.020
A percentage by mass needs two masses, so

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:37.380
use the density.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.460
One litre of cleaner has a mass of

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.700
one thousand and fifty grams.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.260
It holds three point one nine zero moles

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:47.300
of acid, times thirty six point four six

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.260
grams per mole: one hundred and sixteen point

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:51.620
three grams.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.420
That's eleven point one per cent, three significant

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.100
figures, from the density.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.380
The classic mistake is stopping at the aliquot.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.620
Zero point one two seven six moles per

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:07.460
litre is the diluted solution, not the cleaner.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.380
Scale up from the aliquot.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.280
Question three.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.520
An antacid tablet contains sodium hydrogen carbonate, and

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.320
its other ingredients don't react with acid.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.880
A one point three two zero zero gram

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.200
tablet is dissolved and made up to one

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.040
hundred millilitres, and twenty millilitre aliquots are titrated

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.600
with zero point one zero five nine moles

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.360
per litre hydrochloric acid.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.800
Calculate the percentage by mass of sodium hydrogen

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.640
carbonate in the tablet.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.320
Pause here to answer.

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:53.133
No equation is given, so write it.

00:05:53.414 --> 00:05:57.253
Hydrogen carbonate accepts just one proton, making water

00:05:57.414 --> 00:06:00.213
and carbon dioxide, so the ratio is one

00:06:00.294 --> 00:06:00.853
to one.

00:06:01.334 --> 00:06:03.973
The mean titre is exactly twenty three point

00:06:04.054 --> 00:06:06.853
one zero, so the acid is zero point

00:06:07.014 --> 00:06:10.053
one zero five nine times zero point zero

00:06:10.174 --> 00:06:12.933
two three one zero: two point four four

00:06:13.094 --> 00:06:15.813
six times ten to the minus three moles,

00:06:15.854 --> 00:06:17.733
and the aliquot held the same moles of

00:06:17.894 --> 00:06:19.013
hydrogen carbonate.

00:06:21.034 --> 00:06:25.113
Scale up: one hundred millilitres is five aliquots,

00:06:25.194 --> 00:06:27.673
so the tablet held one point two two

00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:29.993
three zero times ten to the minus two

00:06:30.154 --> 00:06:30.953
moles.

00:06:31.274 --> 00:06:33.673
Times eighty four point zero one grams per

00:06:33.754 --> 00:06:36.633
mole, that's one point zero two seven four

00:06:36.794 --> 00:06:37.673
grams.

00:06:37.994 --> 00:06:39.993
Out of one point three two zero zero

00:06:40.154 --> 00:06:42.919
grams, it's seventy seven point eight four per

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:43.673
cent.

00:06:44.154 --> 00:06:46.633
A tablet is weighed directly, so there's no

00:06:47.034 --> 00:06:47.513
density here.

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:52.493
The classic mistake is borrowing carbonate's one to

00:06:52.574 --> 00:06:53.373
two ratio.

00:06:53.854 --> 00:06:55.933
That halves the moles and gives thirty eight

00:06:56.094 --> 00:06:57.853
point nine two per cent.

00:06:58.014 --> 00:06:59.973
Carbonate takes two protons.

00:07:00.174 --> 00:07:03.213
Hydrogen carbonate already has one, so it takes

00:07:03.334 --> 00:07:04.253
only one more.

00:07:06.267 --> 00:07:10.186
So for any titration calculation, follow the moles.

00:07:10.427 --> 00:07:13.386
Use the full molar mass, waters of crystallisation

00:07:13.667 --> 00:07:14.026
included.

00:07:14.507 --> 00:07:17.026
Average only concordant titres.

00:07:17.307 --> 00:07:20.426
Go known to moles, ratio, unknown, with the

00:07:20.507 --> 00:07:22.266
ratio from a balanced equation.

00:07:22.827 --> 00:07:25.226
If the aliquot came from a flask, scale

00:07:25.307 --> 00:07:25.626
up.

00:07:26.107 --> 00:07:29.066
And for a percentage by mass, use masses:

00:07:29.267 --> 00:07:31.386
density for a liquid, the weighed mass for

00:07:31.427 --> 00:07:32.026
a solid.

00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:35.786
Next, which indicator fits a titration.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.240
Here are the key notes.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.400
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:42.280
to copy them.
